 ## Overview
-This family manages the dynamic topology of 3D Gaussian primitives $\mathcal{G}$ during training to balance reconstruction fidelity against computational cost. It solves the problem of under-representation (missing details) and over-representation (redundant or noisy primitives) by periodically inserting, splitting, cloning, or removing Gaussians based on optimization metrics. It differs from static initialization methods by actively adapting the primitive count and distribution throughout the training lifecycle, and from pure pruning methods by including constructive operations (splitting/cloning) to increase local density.
+This family manages the dynamic topology of 3D Gaussian primitives $\mathcal{G}$ during training by adding, splitting, or removing Gaussians to balance reconstruction fidelity against computational cost. It solves the problem of static initialization, where a fixed set of Gaussians cannot adapt to varying scene complexity or optimization progress. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [GroupFlow](6351-groupflow.md) or [3DGS-MCMC](7852-3dgs-mcmc.md) by relying on heuristic metrics (gradient magnitude, opacity, scale) and deterministic splitting/cloning operations rather than probabilistic sampling or graph-based flow, and differs from [VS-Splat Voxel Selection](9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md) by operating directly on the Gaussian primitives rather than voxel grids.
 
